1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,986
A fund manager wants to pick three stocks out of

2
00:00:03,086 --> 00:00:06,780
a shortlist of ten. How many different portfolios could she

3
00:00:06,880 --> 00:00:10,702
end up with? When outcomes are equally likely, probability is

4
00:00:10,802 --> 00:00:13,274
just counting, so we need to count well.

5
00:00:15,366 --> 00:00:18,039
It turns out that almost every counting problem

6
00:00:18,139 --> 00:00:20,282
comes down to one rule, and one trick.

7
00:00:21,366 --> 00:00:25,722
The rule first. A bond gets a rating from one agency, with three

8
00:00:25,822 --> 00:00:30,665
possible grades, and from a second agency, with four. How many pairs of

9
00:00:30,765 --> 00:00:35,607
ratings are possible? Lay them out as a grid: three rows, four columns.

10
00:00:35,700 --> 00:00:39,533
Twelve cells, three times four. That's the m n rule: if one

11
00:00:39,633 --> 00:00:43,467
choice has m options and the next has n, together they have

12
00:00:43,567 --> 00:00:47,067
m times n. With more stages you just keep multiplying.

13
00:00:48,166 --> 00:00:52,589
Now the stocks, but let's first pretend order matters, as if we

14
00:00:52,689 --> 00:00:57,758
were ranking the picks first, second and third. Ten stocks, three slots.

15
00:00:57,850 --> 00:01:01,827
The first slot can take any of the ten. Once it's filled, nine

16
00:01:01,927 --> 00:01:05,707
remain for the second, then eight for the third. By the m n

17
00:01:05,807 --> 00:01:10,967
rule, that's ten times nine times eight: seven hundred and twenty ordered picks.

18
00:01:12,066 --> 00:01:17,057
But a portfolio doesn't care about order. Take one particular trio: C,

19
00:01:17,157 --> 00:01:20,839
G and A. In our list of seven hundred and twenty, it

20
00:01:20,939 --> 00:01:24,766
shows up six times, once for every way of ordering it.

21
00:01:24,866 --> 00:01:28,615
Every trio is counted three factorial, that is six, times.

22
00:01:28,715 --> 00:01:31,401
So we collapse each group of six into one.

23
00:01:31,500 --> 00:01:36,000
Seven hundred and twenty divided by six: one hundred and twenty

24
00:01:36,100 --> 00:01:40,600
possible portfolios. In general, the number of ways to choose r

25
00:01:40,700 --> 00:01:43,886
things from n, ignoring order, is n choose r.

26
00:01:45,483 --> 00:01:49,465
So here's the trick worth remembering. Count as if order mattered,

27
00:01:49,565 --> 00:01:52,373
which is easy with the m n rule. Then divide by

28
00:01:52,473 --> 00:01:57,198
the number of orderings you don't care about. Permutations, combinations, even

29
00:01:57,298 --> 00:02:00,847
partitions into several groups: they are all this one idea.

